      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-250217",
      ":question/problem": "Let $G$ be a graph in which all vertices have even degrees, except for three vertices with degrees $1$, $5$, and $7$. Prove that such a graph cannot exist.\n\nSuppose that graph $G$ with these properties exists, and let $V$ and $E$ be the corresponding sets of vertices and edges. Then, according to the degree sum formula for graphs, we have\n$3$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"
